1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,498
Glancing at a single notification for

2
00:00:02,498 --> 00:00:04,658
thirty seconds does not cost you

3
00:00:04,658 --> 00:00:05,671
thirty seconds.

4
00:00:05,671 --> 00:00:08,169
Gloria Mark's research found it takes

5
00:00:08,169 --> 00:00:10,870
about twenty-three minutes on average to

6
00:00:10,870 --> 00:00:13,030
get back to an interrupted task.

7
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:13,618
In 2009,

8
00:00:13,618 --> 00:00:16,777
business professor Sophie Leroy showed why.

9
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:19,128
She called it attention residue.

10
00:00:19,128 --> 00:00:21,332
When you switch from deep work

11
00:00:21,332 --> 00:00:22,874
to check one message,

12
00:00:22,874 --> 00:00:25,960
part of your attention stays stuck behind.

13
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:27,931
Even after you look back at

14
00:00:27,931 --> 00:00:29,025
your main task,

15
00:00:29,025 --> 00:00:31,361
your brain operates with a steep

16
00:00:31,361 --> 00:00:34,718
cognitive penalty while clearing that leftover

17
00:00:34,718 --> 00:00:35,302
residue.

18
00:00:35,302 --> 00:00:37,491
Four quick checks in two hours

19
00:00:37,491 --> 00:00:39,170
means zero deep output.

20
00:00:39,170 --> 00:00:41,065
Use the Out-of-Sight Protocol:

21
00:00:41,065 --> 00:00:43,085
place your phone inside a closed

22
00:00:43,085 --> 00:00:44,980
drawer in another room for one

23
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:48,011
ninety-minute block every morning before opening

24
00:00:48,011 --> 00:00:48,390
email.

25
00:00:48,390 --> 00:00:51,051
One uninterrupted ninety-minute sprint can beat

26
00:00:51,051 --> 00:00:52,580
a whole fragmented workday.
